WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.569
Every month a forecaster predicts inflation over the next

00:03.669 --> 00:06.917
three months. Each forecast is a tile covering three

00:07.017 --> 00:09.878
months, and the tiles overlap like roof tiles.

00:09.966 --> 00:12.758
Now one surprise lands in a single month: an oil

00:12.858 --> 00:16.011
shock. Every tile that covers that month is wrong, and

00:16.111 --> 00:19.505
wrong in the same direction. One surprise, three mistakes.

00:20.800 --> 00:23.442
That shared mistake is what this lecture is about:

00:23.542 --> 00:27.282
regression with time-series errors, and standard errors that remember.

00:28.366 --> 00:32.932
Mishkin asked whether the Treasury bill rate knows future inflation.

00:33.032 --> 00:37.529
Regress inflation over the next three months on today's three-month

00:37.629 --> 00:41.234
rate. If the market forecasts well, beta is one. Since

00:41.334 --> 00:45.350
nineteen sixty, ordinary least squares gives point five two.

00:46.433 --> 00:49.441
But how precise is that number? Here the overlap returns.

00:50.533 --> 00:54.194
The forecast error over three months is the sum of three monthly

00:54.294 --> 00:57.956
surprises, eta. Next month's error is the sum of the next three.

00:58.266 --> 01:02.291
The two errors share two surprises, so they are correlated. Count

01:02.391 --> 01:06.225
the shared months: three with itself, two one month apart, one

01:06.325 --> 01:10.540
two months apart, none after that. That count is the autocovariance.

01:10.640 --> 01:13.396
The errors are a moving average of order two.

01:16.566 --> 01:21.071
Last lecture's tower tells us the variance that matters:

01:21.171 --> 01:25.017
gamma zero plus twice gamma one plus twice gamma

01:25.117 --> 01:28.553
two. That is three plus four plus two, nine

01:28.653 --> 01:33.733
sigma squared, three times what ordinary least squares assumes.

01:33.833 --> 01:38.015
Notice that every surprise has the same variance: nothing here is

01:38.115 --> 01:42.956
heteroskedastic. The correction comes from the overlap alone. So the honest

01:43.056 --> 01:47.172
standard error is root three, about one point seven three, times

01:47.272 --> 01:50.268
larger, and the interval must stretch to match.

01:51.366 --> 01:54.758
On the real data the residuals echo for longer than

01:54.858 --> 01:58.591
the overlap alone predicts: the blue bars stay high long

01:58.691 --> 02:01.193
after the red MA two pattern has died.

02:01.266 --> 02:06.246
So the standard error grows: point zero three for least squares, twice

02:06.346 --> 02:10.164
that with Newey and West at two lags, three and a half

02:10.264 --> 02:14.953
times with twelve. Even so, one stays outside: the bill rate knows

02:15.053 --> 02:19.452
something about inflation, but it is not an unbiased forecast.

02:21.133 --> 02:24.144
So: when forecast windows overlap, the mistakes are

02:24.244 --> 02:27.073
shared. The errors echo, and the standard errors

02:27.173 --> 02:29.941
must count those echoes, with the HAC sandwich.
